4, 477, 13437, 7682, 1734, 2519, 220, 17, 25, 1556, 7698, 264, 11, 279, 31903, 11, 304, 23819, 824, 2132, 53363, 1734, 2519, 220, 18, 25, 1556, 7698, 865, 11, 279, 6138, 11, 304, 23819, 1734, 1734, 5207, 7338, 77, 2675, 2011, 2612, 264, 925, 449, 279, 4732, 348, 16, 18460, 311, 279, 24379, 7698, 323, 279, 5089, 19180, 555, 264, 3634, 11, 1701, 279, 1890, 5089, 439, 348, 15, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text="Here is a Python solution for the problem:\n\n```python\nv0, v0_unit = input().split()\nv0 = int(v0)\nif v0_unit == 'km/h':\n    v0 = v0 * 1000 / 3600\n\na = int(input())\nx = int(input())\n\nv1 = (v0**2 + 2*a*x)**0.5\n\nif v0_unit == 'km/h':\n    v1 = v1 * 3600 / 1000\n\nv1 = round(v1)\nif v0_unit == 'km/h':\n    v1 = str(int(v1 * 3.6)) + ' km/h'\nelse:\n    v1 = str(v1) + ' m/s'\n\nprint(v1)\n```\n\nThis program first reads the input values for v0, a, and x. It then calculates the new speed v1 using the given formula. The program checks if the original speed v0 is in km/h or m/s and converts it to m/s if necessary. It then calculates v1 in m/s and rounds it to the nearest integer. Finally, it converts v1 back to km/h if necessary and prints the result.", token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 13325, 6425, 369, 279, 3575, 1473, 74694, 12958, 198, 85, 15, 11, 348, 15, 15176, 284, 1988, 1020, 7105, 746, 85, 15, 284, 528, 3832, 15, 340, 333, 348, 15, 15176, 624, 364, 16400, 7682, 3730, 262, 348, 15, 284, 348, 15, 353, 220, 1041, 15, 611, 220, 6843, 15, 271, 64, 284, 528, 5498, 2455, 87, 284, 528, 5498, 12647, 85, 16, 284, 320, 85, 15, 334, 17, 489, 220, 17, 34842, 18988, 33395, 15, 13, 20, 271, 333, 348, 15, 15176, 624, 364, 16400, 7682, 3730, 262, 348, 16, 284, 348, 16, 353, 220, 6843, 15, 611, 220, 1041, 15, 271, 85, 16, 284, 4883, 3832, 16, 340, 333, 348, 15, 15176, 624, 364, 16400, 7682, 3730, 262, 348, 16, 284, 610, 1577, 3832, 1