Output~2025-04-07 00:05:45.890004: [RequestOutput(request_id=683, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Create a program that logs the temperature of the computer and limits its usage depending on the given value. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 4324, 264, 2068, 430, 18929, 279, 9499, 315, 279, 6500, 323, 13693, 1202, 10648, 11911, 389, 279, 2728, 907, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='Here is a Python program that logs the temperature of the computer and limits its usage depending on the given value:\n```\nimport psutil\nimport time\nimport logging\n\n# Set up logging\nlogging.basicConfig(filename=\'temperature_log.txt\', level=logging.INFO)\n\n# Set the maximum allowed temperature in Celsius\nMAX_TEMP = 80\n\nwhile True:\n    # Get the current temperature\n    temp = psutil.sensors_temperatures()[\'coretemp\'][0][1]\n    logging.info(f"Current temperature: {temp}°C")\n\n    # Check if the temperature is above the maximum allowed value\n    if temp > MAX_TEMP:\n        # Limit CPU usage to 50%\n        psutil.cpu_percent(50)\n        logging.warning(f"Temperature exceeded {MAX_TEMP}°C, limiting CPU usage to 50%")\n    else:\n        # Allow normal CPU usage\n        psutil.cpu_percent(100)\n        logging.info("Temperature within normal range, allowing normal CPU usage")\n\n    # Wait for 1 minute before checking again\n    time.sleep(60)\n```\nHere\'s how the program works:\n\n1. It sets up a logging configuration to write log messages to a file named `temperature_log.txt`.\n2. It sets the maximum allowed temperature in Celsius to 80.\n3. It enters an infinite loop that runs every minute.\n4. In each iteration, it gets the current temperature using the `psutil` library.\n5. It logs the current temperature to the log file.\n6. It checks if the temperature is above the maximum allowed value. If it is, it limits CPU usage to 50% using `psutil.cpu_percent(50)`. It also logs a warning message.\n7. If the temperature is within the normal range, it allows normal CPU usage (100%) and logs an info message.\n8. It waits for 1 minute before checking again.\n\nNote that this program assumes that the `psutil` library is installed and configured correctly on your system. You may need to install it using `pip install psutil` if it\'s not already installed. Also, this program only monitors the temperature of the CPU core, so you may want to modify it to monitor other temperatures (e.g., GPU, hard drive) if needed.', token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 13325, 2068, 430, 18929, 279, 9499, 315, 279, 6500, 323, 13693, 1202, 10648, 11911, 389, 279, 2728, 907, 512, 14196, 4077, 475, 4831, 1338, 198, 475, 892, 198, 475, 8558, 271, 2, 2638, 709, 8558, 198, 26330, 69897, 11202, 1151, 35658, 5337, 3996, 518, 2237, 71328, 39417, 696, 2, 2638, 279, 7340, 5535, 9499, 304, 62447, 198, 10809, 42344, 284, 220, 1490, 271, 3556, 3082, 512, 262, 674, 2175, 279, 1510, 9499, 198, 262, 2798, 284, 4831, 1338, 516, 27627, 29713, 716, 2859, 39715, 2202, 3975, 10136, 15, 1483, 16, 933, 262, 8558, 5506, 968, 1, 5520, 9499, 25, 314, 3975, 92, 11877, 34, 5240, 262, 674, 4343, 422, 279, 9499, 374, 3485, 279, 7340, 5535, 907, 198, 262, 422, 2798, 871, 8498, 42344, 512, 286, 674, 29108, 14266, 10648, 311, 220, 1135, 14062, 286, 4831, 1338, 43487, 30983, 7, 1135, 340, 286, 8558, 29936, 968, 1, 41790, 34908, 314, 10809, 42344, 92, 11877, 34, 11, 33994, 14266, 10648, 311, 220, 1135, 4, 1158, 262, 775, 512, 286, 674, 27628, 4725, 14266, 10648, 198, 286, 4831, 13