Output~2025-04-07 00:26:59.576305: [RequestOutput(request_id=1162, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Explain the difference between the Southern and Northern Lights. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 83017, 279, 6811, 1990, 279, 16642, 323, 17355, 35270, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text="The Southern and Northern Lights, also known as the Aurora Australis and Aurora Borealis, respectively, are natural light displays that occur in the Earth's polar regions. While they share some similarities, there are distinct differences between the two.\n\n**Location:**\nThe Northern Lights (Aurora Borealis) are typically visible in the Northern Hemisphere, primarily at high latitudes such as Alaska, Canada, Norway, Sweden, Finland, and Russia. The Southern Lights (Aurora Australis) are visible in the Southern Hemisphere, primarily in Antarctica, Australia, New Zealand, and parts of South America and Africa.\n\n**Causes:**\nThe Northern Lights are caused by charged particles from the sun interacting with the Earth's magnetic field and atmosphere. The particles are drawn towards the poles, causing the atoms and molecules in the atmosphere to become excited, leading to the emission of light. The Southern Lights are caused by the same process, but the particles come from a different direction, as the Earth's magnetic field is tilted at an angle of about 11 degrees from the plane of the solar system.\n\n**Appearance:**\nThe Northern Lights are generally more frequent and intense than the Southern Lights, with more vibrant colors and a wider range of colors, including green, blue, and red. The Southern Lights tend to be less frequent and less intense, with a more limited color palette, often appearing as a diffuse glow or a faint, wispy curtain of light.\n\n**Seasonality:**\nThe Northern Lights are typically visible from late August to early April, with the best viewing times around the equinoxes in March and September. The Southern Lights are visible from March to September, with the best viewing times around the equinoxes in March and September.\n\n**Frequency:**\nThe Northern Lights are more frequent and widespread than the Southern Lights, with an average of 240 nights per year in which the lights are visible in the Northern Hemisphere. The Southern Lights are less frequent, with an average of 40 nights per year in which the lights are visible in the Southern Hemisphere.\n\nIn summary, while both the Northern and Southern Lights are breathtaking natural phenomena, they differ in terms of location, causes, appearance, seasonality, and frequency. The Northern Lights are more frequent, intense, and colorful, while the Southern Lights are less frequent and less intense, but still a remarkable sight to behold.", token_ids=[791, 16642, 323, 17355, 35270, 11, 1101, 3967, 439, 279, 47892, 6126, 285, 323, 47892, 426, 461, 35965, 11, 15947, 11, 527, 5933, 3177, 19207, 430, 12446, 304, 279, 9420, 596, 25685, 13918, 13, 6104, 814, 4430, 1063, 43874, 11, 1070, 527, 12742, 12062, 1990, 279, 1403, 382, 334, 4812, 25, 1035, 791, 17355, 35270, 320, 32, 324, 6347, 426, 461, 35965, 8, 527, 11383, 9621, 304, 279, 17355, 96878, 11, 15871, 520, 1579, 6987, 21237, 1778, 439, 28366, 11, 7008, 11, 32603, 11, 24067, 11, 37355, 11, 323, 8524, 13, 578, 16642, 35270, 320, 32, 324, 6347, 6126, 285, 8, 527, 9621, 304, 279, 16642, 96878, 11, 15871, 304, 72787, 11, 8494, 11, 1561, 17340, 11, 323, 5596, 315, 4987, 5270, 323, 10384, 382, 334, 23389, 488