Output~2025-04-07 00:49:39.776040: [RequestOutput(request_id=1654, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Write a bash script to print the size of a file in human-readable format (e.g. 1.5K, 4M, 2G). ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 9842, 264, 28121, 5429, 311, 1194, 279, 1404, 315, 264, 1052, 304, 3823, 84318, 3645, 320, 68, 1326, 13, 220, 16, 13, 20, 42, 11, 220, 19, 44, 11, 220, 17, 38, 570, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='Here is a bash script that prints the size of a file in human-readable format:\n```bash\n#!/bin/bash\n\n# Check if a file is provided as an argument\nif [ $# -ne 1 ]; then\n  echo "Usage: $0 <file_path>"\n  exit 1\nfi\n\nFILE_SIZE=$(stat -c%s "$1")\n\n# Define the units and their corresponding sizes\nUNITS=(K M G T P)\nSIZES=(1024 1048576 1073741824 1099511627776 1125899906842624)\n\n# Find the appropriate unit for the file size\nfor ((i=0; i<${#SIZES[@]}; i++)); do\n  if [ ${FILE_SIZE} -ge ${SIZES[$i]} ]; then\n    UNIT=${UNITS[$i]}\n    break\n  fi\ndone\n\n# Calculate the human-readable size\nHUMAN_READABLE_SIZE=$(printf "%.2f\\n" "${FILE_SIZE}/${SIZES[$i]}")\n\n# Print the result\necho "${HUMAN_READABLE_SIZE}${UNIT}"\n```\nLet me explain how the script works:\n\n1. The script checks if a file is provided as an argument using the `$#` variable, which returns the number of command-line arguments. If no file is provided, it prints an error message and exits.\n2. The `stat` command is used to get the size of the file in bytes.\n3. The script defines an array of units (K, M, G, T, P) and their corresponding sizes in bytes.\n4. The script loops through the array of units and sizes to find the appropriate unit for the file size.\n5. The script calculates the human-readable size by dividing the file size by the appropriate unit size and formatting the result as a floating-point number with two decimal places.\n6. Finally, the script prints the human-readable size followed by the appropriate unit.\n\nYou can save this script to a file (e.g., `filesize.sh`), make it executable with `chmod +x filesize.sh`, and then run it with the file path as an argument, like this: `./filesize.sh /path/to/file`.', token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 28121, 5429, 430, 24370, 279, 1404, 315, 264, 1052, 304, 3823, 84318, 3645, 512, 74694, 47316, 198, 8872, 7006, 17587, 271, 2, 4343, 422, 264, 1052, 374, 3984, 439, 459, 5811, 198, 333, 510, 67015, 482, 818, 220, 16, 13385, 1243, 198, 220, 1722, 330, 15126, 25, 400, 15, 366, 1213, 2703, 19681, 220, 4974, 220, 16, 198, 10188, 271, 6169, 4190, 16162, 10079, 482, 66, 13249, 5312, 16, 5240, 2, 19127, 279, 8316, 323, 872, 12435, 12562, 198, 1899, 14813, 4640, 42, 386, 480, 350, 393, 340, 50, 90515, 4640, 4278, 19, 220, 6849, 20907, 21, 220, 7699, 18265, 10828, 19, 220, 7743, 24989, 10674, 15831, 21, 220, 7261, 22905, 19146, 24313, 14274, 19, 696, 2, 7531, 279, 8475, 5089, 369, 279, 1052, 1404, 198, 2000, 1819, 72, 28, 15, 26, 602, 27, 2420, 2, 50, 90515, 12606, 60, 11308, 602, 1044, 6030, 656, 198, 220, 422, 510, 3654, 6169, 4190, 92, 482, 713, 3654, 50, 90515, 3525, 72, 14316, 13385, 1243, 198, 262, 39302, 12866, 1899, 14813, 3525, 72, 24333, 262, 1464, 198, 220, 9314, 198, 10655, 271, 2, 21157, 279, 3823, 84318, 1404, 198, 39, 68665, 13418, 3578, 4190, 16162, 2578, 67721, 17, 69, 1734, 1, 11094, 6169, 4190, 23650, 50, 90515, 3525, 72, 14316, 5240, 2, 8377, 279, 1121, 198, 3123, 11094, 39, 68665, 13418, 3578, 4190, 32292, 23909, 11444, 14196, 4077, 10267, 757, 10552, 1268, 279